---
const code = `public class DriveNode extends Node {
    DriveNode(Orchestrator orch, SafeDevice<DcMotorEx> left, SafeDevice<DcMotorEx> right) {
        super(orch);
        this.left = left;
        this.right = right;
    }
    private final SafeDevice<DcMotorEx> left, right;

    @RunPeriodically(hz = 50, hardware = true)
    public void drive() {
        double y = orchestrator.getLatestValue("g1/left_stick_y", Double.class).orElse(0.0);
        double r = orchestrator.getLatestValue("g1/right_stick_y", Double.class).orElse(0.0);
        left.run(m -> m.setPower(-y));
        right.run(m -> m.setPower(-r));
    }
}`;
---
<section class="live reveal">
  <div class="live__head">
    <h2>What a node actually looks like</h2>
    <p>Annotated methods get wired at register time. No boilerplate glue, no static registry.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="live__panel">
    <header class="live__bar">
      <span class="dot" style="background:#ff5f56"></span>
      <span class="dot" style="background:#ffbd2e"></span>
      <span class="dot" style="background:#27c93f"></span>
      <span class="live__filename">DriveNode.java</span>
    </header>
    <pre><code>{code}</code></pre>
  </div>
  <ul role="list" class="live__notes">
    <li><strong>@RunPeriodically(hz=50, hardware=true)</strong> — this loop runs on the single hardware thread, so motor writes are serial.</li>
    <li><strong>getLatestValue(...).orElse(0.0)</strong> — pull a previously-published gamepad axis without subscribing.</li>
    <li><strong>SafeDevice.run(...)</strong> — schedules the motor write on the same hardware thread. No off-thread setPower calls.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

